{
  "$comment": "VAPP-87: the OVERLAY placement contract (src/overlay.ts placeOverlay): an anchor box, the overlay's size and the viewport, a preferred side → where it lands. OVERLAY_OFFSET (4 px) from the anchor, centred on the cross axis, or (VAPP-276 `align`, default centre) flush with the anchor's START or END edge along it (top-start, top-end, bottom-start…; the core mirrors start/end under RTL); FLIP to the opposite side when the preferred side has less room than the overlay needs and the opposite has more; SHIFT on the cross axis to stay OVERLAY_PADDING (8 px) inside the viewport. `expected` is what the TS rule returns (src/overlay.test.ts replays it); the React renderer checks that Radix's popper lands within `tolerancePx` of it in headless Chromium (browser/overlay.test.ts), the Rust core's layer manager (VAPP-86) reproduces it exactly. VAPP-276: `byId` cases open a Popover with NO trigger whose `anchor` prop names a node elsewhere in the tree (a bottom-panel button): the layer is placed against THAT node's frame, the painters replay them with the anchor as a sibling. VAPP-281: `hosted` = a popup hosted OUTSIDE its surface (the surface's `hostedPopups` setting; eDE maps it to an xdg_popup): `anchorPosition` places it against the anchor in SURFACE coordinates at its unclipped size with NO flip and NO shift (the host's window system constrains it), so it may land outside `surface`. `matchAnchorWidth` cases (Popover `matchAnchorWidth: true`): the panel is EXACTLY its anchor's width, so `size.width` = `anchor.width` (the overlay's size, what the placement rule gets); the renderers paint the content box at `contentWidth` (narrower than the anchor) and assert the placed panel's width = the anchor's (the IDE's `/` typeahead at the composer card's full width).",
